# Elixir Koans - 06 Lists

```elixir
import ExUnit.Assertions
```

## We can see what is ahead

```elixir
assert List.first([1, 2, 3]) == ___
```

## Checking what's trailing is also simple

```elixir
assert List.last([1, 2, 3]) == ___
```

## Lists can store anything you throw at them

```elixir
assert [1, 2] ++ [:a, "b"] == ___
```

## Things can evolve

```elixir
assert [1, 2, 3] -- [3] == ___
```

## Evolution can have different forms

```elixir
assert List.delete([:a, :b, :c], :b) == ___
```

## Precision is also valued

```elixir
assert List.delete_at([:a, :b, :c], 2) == ___
```

## Replication is also possible

```elixir
assert List.duplicate("life", 3) == ___
```

## Sometimes leveling the playing field is desired

```elixir
assert List.flatten([1, [2, 3], 4, [5]]) == ___
```

## Order can also be specified for new members

```elixir
assert List.insert_at([1, 2, 3], 1, 4) == ___
```

## We can replace things at specified positions

```elixir
assert List.replace_at([1, 2, 3], 0, 10) == ___
```

## When a replacement cannot be found, the list remains the same

```elixir
assert List.replace_at([1, 2, 3], 10, 0) == ___
```

## Order is bound by nature's laws

```elixir
assert List.insert_at([1, 2, 3], 10, 4) == ___
```

## Sometimes it's faster to loop around back

```elixir
assert List.insert_at([1, 2, 3], -1, 4) == ___
```

## We can also transform ourselves completely

```elixir
assert List.to_tuple([1, 2, 3]) == ___
```

## Wrapping other values is a handy option

```elixir
assert List.wrap("value") == ___
```

## Wrapping nothing produces a list of nothing

```elixir
assert List.wrap(nil) == ___
```

## When there is already a list do not wrap it again

```elixir
assert List.wrap(["value"]) == ___
```

